\label{fig:error_vs_k}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Structure.}
One general factor dominates (63\% of the variance of the standardised scores, the second 13\%), and beyond it the spectrum decays slowly: the rank estimators we set out before the results disagree (5 to 10, about 16 and 56; Figure~\ref{fig:spectrum}, Appendix~\ref{app:further}), so we claim no number of factors and choose the rank by inner cross-validation at each $k$.

\paragraph{How many benchmarks are needed.}
With the factor model and designed selection, the RMSE on the target set falls from 10.1 points [9.5, 10.8] at $k=1$ to 7.1 at 5, 6.4 at 20 and 5.4 at 109, against a floor of 5.1 points (Figure~\ref{fig:error_vs_k}a). Predicting every score as the mean of the revealed ones (P1) stays above 9.5 points and the benchmark mean is at 15.4. The excess over the floor is 2.1 points at $k=5$, 1.3 at 20, 0.7 at 50 and 0.2 at 80 (Figure~\ref{fig:error_vs_k}b), so ``how many'' has a curve for an answer: the smallest $k$ whose excess is at most 2 points is 8 [5, 12], at most $\delta_{\text{floor}}=1.17$ points (the secondary margin) is 32 [20, 50], and at most 1 point is 50 [32, 50]. The pre-set margin $\delta\approx0.1$ point is not reached on the grid up to 109 benchmarks: adjacent models differ by far less than the error of predicting a score from all of the model's others. The floor, and with it $k^*$, depends on the capacity of the model that defines it: the 32 is for the floor at the rank chosen by inner cross-validation (16 in the first fold). On the same 20 splits, with the rank fixed at 4, 8 or 12 the floor is 6.2, 5.6 and 5.2 points against 5.0, $k^*$ at $\delta_{\text{floor}}$ is 3, 8 and 20 against 32, and the error at $k=20$ at rank 12 is 0.5 points [0.3, 0.7] below that of the cross-validated rank, which is chosen without the test models (Appendix~\ref{app:checks}). On the temporal and strength splits (13 and 22 models) the floor is 10.0 and 7.6 points and $k^*$ at $\delta_{\text{floor}}$ is 3 [3, 8] and 20 [20, 32].

